<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pfalzgrafenstein Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1327">1327</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pfalzgrafenstein Castle is a toll castle on a small island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaub">Kaub</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Louis IV the Bavarian built its tower in 1326–1327 to collect river tolls, and a surrounding curtain wall was added soon after. It remained a toll station of the counts palatine and their successors into the nineteenth century, and is now part of the Upper Middle Rhine Valley <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pfalzgrafenstein-castle.jpg" alt="Pfalzgrafenstein Castle" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jörg Braukmann · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Burg_Pfalzgrafenstein_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The castle stands on Falkenau island. Its central tower is pentagonal, 36 metres high with six storeys, shaped to break waves and ice since it first stood alone. The outer wall, 2.60 metres thick, gives the castle a footprint of 51 by 21 metres. To force payment, chain booms were raised across the narrow river passage; a ship that refused was seized and its crew held in the dungeon, a wooden raft at the bottom of a well, until a ransom was paid. The wider channel could not be used because of dangerous water downstream, and the tolls themselves were paid at a customs house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaub">Kaub</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The tower threatened the tolls that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> collected at the Mouse Tower at Bingen and at Stolzenfels Castle, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xxii">Pope John XXII</a> urged them to excommunicate King Ludwig IV the Bavarian and act against the toll at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaub">Kaub</a>, though nothing was done. The dispute probably led Ludwig to add the six-sided wall around the tower in about 1340. In 1339 he gave the castle to his nephew Rudolph II, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a>. In 1504, during the War of the Succession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a>, it withstood a 39-day siege by Landgrave William II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> unscathed.</p><p>Elector Friedrich IV added a gun bastion in 1607, making it the first Middle Rhine castle adapted to modern guns. Spanish forces under General Spinola took it without a fight in 1620 and held it until Hessian forces forced their surrender in January 1632. The baroque tower cap was added in 1714. In 1803 the castle passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-nassau">House of Nassau</a>, and on 1 January 1814 Russian engineers built a pontoon bridge by the island for Field-Marshal von Blücher's army of 60,000 men to cross in pursuit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> annexed Nassau in 1866, toll collection ended in 1867.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The castle has belonged to the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a> since 1946 and was restored between 1967 and 1975. It is a public museum, reached by ferry from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaub">Kaub</a>, and lies in the Rhine Gorge, added to the UNESCO World Heritage list in June 2002. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a> described it as a stone ship, eternally floating on the Rhine.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pfalzgrafenstein Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the vineyards of the Haardt and the Rhine valley, and especially from the Rhine tolls collected at places such as Bacharach and Kaub, where the island toll castle of <b>Pfalzgrafenstein</b> still stands, which made the electors among the richest princes of the Empire in the later Middle Ages.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
